    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study compares downstream benchmark scores for MoVA variants with different LLM sizes in a multimodal setting; it does not test the original pretraining-loss scaling laws.",
    "gate_note": "The paper uses language-modeling loss to construct expert-routing annotations, but does not report loss-versus-parameter, token, or compute scaling fits. Its benchmark comparisons across model sizes bear on V6; other benchmark results are not treated as tests of the loss-scaling versions.",
    "test_reason": "This is a method paper for a multimodal model that routes between vision experts. Its only size comparison swaps whole LLM backbones from different families: Vicuna-7B, Llama3-8B and Hermes-Yi-34B. Total parameters are 10B, 11B and 38B. The authors call this validating MoVA's 'scaling property', but they fit no curve, make no prediction and control nothing besides size. There are only three points, and the backbones differ in pretraining data and recipe. So it does not test whether downstream performance improves predictably with scale or loss (V6), and no loss scaling is reported (V1–V5, V7). The benchmark trend is recorded under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that adaptive routing and fusion of vision experts yields state-of-the-art multimodal results. They use the 34B variant to 'validate the scaling property of MoVA', and say expert ensembles are 'an effective dimension for MLLM model scaling'. They make no claim about power-law loss scaling.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains a multimodal vision-language model with routed vision experts and compares downstream benchmarks; it does not fit language-model loss scaling laws or optimize compute allocation.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported downstream score trends vary by benchmark; the compared MoVA variants use different LLM backbones (Vicuna, Llama3, and Hermes-Yi).",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "MoVA variants with larger and different LLM backbones (10B, 11B, 38B total) mostly score higher on Table 3–5 benchmarks. Exceptions: QBench is flat, POPE dips at 38B, and VQAv2/GQA fall at 38B in Table 4 (83.5→82.3, 65.2→63.9).",
        "proposed_claim": "Swapping in a larger LLM backbone in an MLLM generally raises downstream benchmark scores, but not uniformly across benchmarks.",
